True, but not really what I'm talking about.

I'd just like to note here that all non-cheating ascensions are significant 
and worthy achievements in their own right. My discussion here is intended 
to look at the relative merits of various ways of ending up with an 
unbroken conduct having ascended. I don't mean to denigrate any ascender's 
achievement, which I know is quite significant.

My point is, if you aim to ascend only and on one or more of your 
successful ascensions, find you don't happen to need/be able to do anything 
to break a particular conduct, your game in fact comes from a different 
population of ascension paths than if you choose to avoid breaking a 
conduct from turn one. Those populations intersect, but are different. Thus 
you are aiming for a different thing.

By aiming for an ascension only, you have a wider choice of paths, many of 
which are easier, and some of which happen not to break conducts. By being 
prepared to break a chosen conduct at all, you are automatically choosing 
from a wider initial set of possible paths. The later you choose to follow 
a conduct, and the smaller the provocation that will lead you to break it, 
the broader the choice is. Thus the ascension is taken from a larger and 
easier population, no matter what the final unbroken conducts are.

By choosing to adhere to a conduct from turn 1, and sticking to it, you are 
maximally restricting the population of acceptable outcomes, using that 
particular ascension condition.

As each ascension is by definition unique, it can only be made sense of in 
relation to the population it comes from. As an ascension decided on 100% 
from turn one comes from a smaller population of paths that are on average 
harder, it means more than an ascension that comes from a broader set of 
ascension paths, some of which include not breaking the conduct - the 
harder conduct-keeping paths are weeded out, and replaced by easier, 
conduct-breaking ones. Thus the conduct is devalued by the preparedness to 
break it, as the population of ascensions is easier, even if you disregard 
the conduct-breaking ones.


